<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="skill">tools</ansi>

Working the wild takes the right tool. Skinning a carcass, butchering it,
felling a tree or sawing a log all depend mostly on your body and your tool,
and only a little on practice.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Tool Types ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">knife</ansi>          skinning and cutting meat
  <ansi fg="yellow">cleaver</ansi>        butchering through joints for bone and fat
  <ansi fg="yellow">bone saw</ansi>       horn, antler and tusk
  <ansi fg="yellow">axe</ansi>            felling trees
  <ansi fg="yellow">saw</ansi>            ripping logs into planks, staves and shafts
  <ansi fg="yellow">scraper</ansi>        fleshing hides before curing
  <ansi fg="yellow">sickle</ansi>         carried while you forage, finds more and rarer herbs
  <ansi fg="yellow">carving knife</ansi>  bone, horn and fine woodwork (any whittling knife)
  <ansi fg="yellow">pick</ansi>           mining ore, coal and gems (<ansi fg="command">help mining</ansi>)

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Tool Quality ━━━</ansi>

A better tool works better and brings home better material. The tier caps
the best grade you can take, and raises your odds of rare parts and herbs:

  <ansi fg="yellow">crude</ansi>        up to standard   half the usual chance of a rare part
  <ansi fg="yellow">iron</ansi>         up to fine       the usual chance
  <ansi fg="yellow">steel</ansi>        up to superb     half again; trophy parts need steel
  <ansi fg="yellow">masterwork</ansi>   up to pristine   double; a great bear pelt needs masterwork

The hardest woods want a good axe too: yew and black walnut need iron, and
ironwood needs steel. The harder ores want a good pick: silver, basalt-iron
and lake-iron need iron, and gold needs steel.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Getting Tools ━━━</ansi>

Merchants sell only <ansi fg="yellow">crude</ansi> tools: a flint knife, a camp hatchet, a
butcher's chopper, a rough saw, a flint scraper or sickle, a rough pick.
Hunters, trappers, lumber camps and smiths carry them: Hunter Delk at
Hunter's Hollow in Pothole Coulee, Trapper Maudry at the Stillwater
travelers' camp, Trapper Ottar at the Fernway's eastern trailhead,
Camp-Foreman Bertt at the Cascade Pass lumber camp, Woodcutter Hagen on the
North Road, Corwin the Tanner in New Plymouth and Corwin Ashlade in Amber
Valley. Rough picks come from Mine Foreman Dagna at the Pothole Coulee mine,
Smith Rusk and Smith Brindle. They also buy what you bring back.

Iron, steel and masterwork tools are made only by smiths (<ansi fg="command">help blacksmithing</ansi>).
A forged tool comes out graded: a pristine iron tool works like steel, a
crude steel one like iron. A one-handed dagger or sword will do as a crude
knife, and a hatchet as a crude axe or cleaver.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Wear ━━━</ansi>

Every job wears a tool a little. A crude tool lasts about thirty jobs, iron
eighty, steel a hundred and sixty and masterwork over three hundred, longer
for a well-graded one. A tool shows <ansi fg="item-quality">(worn)</ansi> and then <ansi fg="item-quality">(badly worn)</ansi>, and
when it shows <ansi fg="red">(broken)</ansi> it cannot be used until it is mended
(<ansi fg="command">help repair</ansi>). Weapons pressed into service as tools do not wear this way.

Some recipes need a tool too: a scraper to cure a hide, a knife to cut
leather, a carving knife to work bone. A recipe made with a tool, or from
graded materials, comes out graded itself, and the tool caps that grade.
You only need to carry a tool; the best one you have is used.

